    <abstract id="aspace_fab6d82ffcbc03d82c6756710f9916fb">This collection documents the demolition of Penn Station from 1963-1966, including photographs of the station's interior and exterior photographs taken from a bird's-eye view across the street at the Hotel Pennsylvania.</abstract>
    <physloc id="aspace_07ee1aadc6d0a74ef0190c4c3afe413f">Materials in this collection may be stored offsite. For more information on making arrangements to consult them, please visit www.nyhistory.org/library/visit.</physloc>
    <langmaterial id="aspace_f16a6634dca493a0173e36de006f0132">This is a collection of photographs, but any text is in English.</langmaterial>
  </did>
  <arrangement id="aspace_9b9e546060dcbe9dbf206606f973f5c0">
    <head>Arrangement</head>
<p>The photographs are in the order in which they were received from the photographer.</p>  </arrangement>
  <bioghist id="aspace_60a62877fd73e3f617235f22123ba906">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Norman McGrath is an English-American photographer who specializes in architecture and interiors. He was born in 1931 in London and grew up in Dublin, where he studied engineering at Trinity College. A few years after graduating, McGrath moved to New York with the intent of becoming a structural engineer; after being fired from his engineering job of four years, he decided to take up photography as a part-time vocation alongside other engineering work.<lb/></p><p>During his transition to photography in the early 1960s, McGrath worked as an engineer at the Hotel Pennsylvania, which sat across 7th Avenue from Penn Station. During a workshop with Harper's Bazaar art director Alexey Brodovitch, he chose to start a project documenting the station's gradual demolition over the course of the 1960s. His bird's-eye view from the hotel allowed him expansive exterior shots.<lb/></p><p>His photographs of Penn Station established him as an architectural photographer, and he has continued to work in architectural photography. His photos have appeared in architectural publications, commercial projects, and magazines. In 1985, the American Institute of Architects selected him for its Institute Honor. </p>  </bioghist>
