<p>The photographs were transferred to archival folders in groups of 10 photographs.</p>  </processinfo>
  <scopecontent id="aspace_694ae3db82f8bd150f739629e89116dd">
    <head>Scope and Contents</head>
<p>This collection includes 50 photographs McGrath took while documenting the gradual demolition of New York's Penn Station between 1963 and 1966. The original Pennsylvania Station, completed in 1910, was a Beaux-Arts masterpiece designed by McKim, Mead and White. By the early 1960s, the station's deterioriation, high operating cost, and lower train ridership led to its replacement with a smaller underground station and Madison Square Garden at street level. Photographs in the collection include interior and exterior shots exhibiting the scale of the McKim, Mead and White building in all stages of deconstruction. Specific areas of interest include the Waiting Hall, the Concourse, and the Arcade.<lb/></p><p>McGrath made these prints in the 2020s from scans of his original negatives and slides, and many of the photographs include information about the original camera and film and details of how he reprinted the image. Some photographs were part of an exhibit McGrath did in 2024 showcasing the demolition. Titles and dates are taken from McGrath's notes on the verso of each photograph; he also signed each photograph on the verso.</p>  </scopecontent>
  <otherfindaid id="aspace_937958a7edcaa0bb63f918dd867729e0">
